  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe core idea is to transform the discrete optimum finding into a continuous optimization problem on a torus, leveraging periodic functions. The key insight is that the optimal h(x) likely exhibits periodic behavior over [0,2]. We represent h(x) with a Fourier series truncated at frequency N_max=7, yielding coefficients c_0, ..., c_7. This parameterization allows using continuous optimization methods on a bounded space with smooth constraints. The periodic nature naturally handles circular shifts, which aligns with the convolution structure of the overlap integral.\n\nThe primary limitation is that we assume a Fourier truncation of order 7 captures the essential structure. However, this is justified by extant literature in approximation theory, which shows Fourier expansions provide excellent approximations for periodic functions with controlled error bounds. The distinction between this approach and previous ones is that it directly models periodicity constraints mathematically rather than relying on discretization or combinatorial heuristics.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nDefine the Fourier series representation:\nh(x) = Re(\u2211_{k=0}^{7} c_k e^{2\u03c0i\u202fk\u202fx}\nwith c_0 being real.\n\nConstraints:\n- Fourier coefficients c_k (complex), including c_0 real\n- \u222b_0^2 h(x)dx=1 (enforced via Lagrange multiplier or projection)\n\nObjective function:\nJ(h) = (1/dx) * max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nhimplicitly defined by its Fourier coefficients\n\nThe maximum is approximated using mode='full' correlation in the discrete scoring validation.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n    a. Parameterization:\n    Represent the real part of each complex coefficient requires 2N_max-1 real parameters (c_0 real, conjugate symmetry for others)\n\n    b. Optimization setup:\n    - Input(h_init) of length 600 converted to Fourier coefficients\n    - Use forward-backward splitting with 128 iterations per function eval\n    - Targets: \u03b1=32, \u03f5=1e-3, step_size=0.2\n    - Primary function: Smooth max-correlation version (LogSumExp)\n    - Check feasibility (0<=h<=1 and \u222bhdx=1) using entropy projection\n\n    c. Phase 1: Fourier interpolation:\n    Convert parent's values to Fourier coefficients at degree N_max=7\n    \n    d. Phase 2: Entropic projection-based gradient flow:\n    - Initialize using projected samples\n    - Perform entropy-regularized gradient descent (using Sinkhorn)\n    - Calculate score with correlation function\n    - Update every 100 steps (projection)\n\n    e. Phase 3: Post-Processing:\n    Re-convert to grid points and validate against original integral and constraints\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n    a. Fourier Basis Computing:\n    Precompute exponential terms using FFT to handle the continuous evaluation of optimal transportation costs.\n\n    b. Entropy Handling:\n    Adopt entropy=0.1 as standard. Fallback measure: Iterative clipping (h.clip(0,1)) used if diverges.\n\n    c. Dimension Handling:\n    If dimension mismatch encountered, use spectral projection back to the function space.\n\n    d. Operator Application:\n    Since optimization targets beam search over Fourier space, gradient descent requires careful projection secondary constraints.\n\n    e. Checkpointing:\n    Store best solution every 100 steps and use median preservation.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n    Time allocation: 90% optimization duration, 10% validation\n    Failure handling: Use lag-based gradient safe-guard (||\u2207J|| <= 1e-4)\n    Numerical fallback: If feasible projection fails, use brute-force penalty found in sinkhorn rsample.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n    a. Final Candidate Verification:\n    - \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1e-6 (absolute error)\n    - 0 <= h(x) <= 1 for all x\n    - Correlation score computed with np.correlate using mode='full'\n\n    b. Score Calculation:\n    For solution h, compute: (max_k correlation)[k] * dx\n\n    c. Format Output:\n    (h_grid_values, score_value, grid_points=1200)\n\n    d. F Specification: Return the grid's min and max values along with unconstrained representation.\n\n    Note: In case of divergence, use Noether's theorem variant to ensure periodicity.\n\n    Algorithm Details:\n    - Projected gradient descent (30,000-time steps)\n    - Sinkhorn regularized gradients with temperature 0.01, then increased to 0.1\n    - Subproblem evaluations: 64 gradient estimates\n    - Early termination condition: Consecutive 20 steps no change",
